WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.323
Picture a family sharing one account in a payments app, with 100.00 in it.

2
00:00:07.323 --> 00:00:12.633
Two of them pay at the same second: 50.00 for groceries, and 30.00 for a bike repair.

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.344
There's enough for both.

4
00:00:14.344 --> 00:00:16.089
The bike repair goes through.

5
00:00:16.089 --> 00:00:21.987
The groceries come back at once, with an error: someone else is busy with this account, try again.

6
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:25.780
Why didn't it just wait its turn, like a queue at a counter?

7
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.495
Waiting feels fair, and safe.

8
00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:30.839
Last lesson, waiting was exactly the weak spot.

9
00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:42.607
When one payment touches records on two machines, a coordinator, the program in charge, asks each machine to promise, writes its decision in its log, and then tells them to commit.

10
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:50.921
And if the coordinator crashes after the promises, the machines wait, holding their records locked, until that one program comes back.

11
00:00:50.921 --> 00:00:53.215
Everyone in the queue waits with them.

12
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:57.440
Today's database is DynamoDB, Amazon's database service.

13
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.827
You store items, records you look up by a key, and Amazon spreads them over many machines.

14
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:11.044
Each slice of the data is called a partition, and lives on its own machine, a storage node.

15
00:01:11.044 --> 00:01:13.791
The family's account is item A-1001.

16
00:01:13.791 --> 00:01:17.606
The bike shop is A-2002, and the grocer is A-3003.

17
00:01:17.606 --> 00:01:19.743
Three items, three machines.

18
00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:26.096
DynamoDB takes a list of changes in one request, and applies all of them, or none.

19
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:28.384
So how does it avoid the waiting?

20
00:01:28.384 --> 00:01:32.357
Picture a travel agent booking a flight and a hotel for one trip.

21
00:01:32.357 --> 00:01:36.819
She asks each to hold a booking, and a held seat can't go to anyone else.

22
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:41.032
When both are held, she writes confirmed in the file, and tells both.

23
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:42.383
Here's the twist.

24
00:01:42.383 --> 00:01:47.098
If the seat is already held for another trip, the airline says no at once.

25
00:01:47.098 --> 00:01:48.118
No waiting list.

26
00:01:48.118 --> 00:01:54.617
And the file sits in a shared cabinet, so if she goes home sick, any colleague can finish the booking.

27
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:55.784
Let's map it.

28
00:01:55.784 --> 00:01:57.335
The hold is a prepare.

29
00:01:57.335 --> 00:01:59.451
The file is DynamoDB's ledger.

30
00:01:59.451 --> 00:02:08.056
And the colleague is another coordinator, because Amazon runs a whole fleet of them, and any one can take any transaction.

31
00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:10.100
The picture breaks on time.

32
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.390
A hold lasts a day.

33
00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:14.175
Here, the whole thing takes milliseconds.

34
00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:16.078
Now the two payments.

35
00:02:16.078 --> 00:02:19.529
T1, the bike repair, goes to coordinator one.

36
00:02:19.529 --> 00:02:22.828
T2, the groceries, goes to coordinator two.

37
00:02:23.067 --> 00:02:24.460
Phase one is prepare.

38
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:28.509
Coordinator one asks the family's node to check three things.

39
00:02:28.509 --> 00:02:30.301
Is the balance at least 30?

40
00:02:30.301 --> 00:02:33.819
Is this transaction newer than the item's last write?

41
00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:35.943
And is nobody else busy with it?

42
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:40.811
All three hold, so the node says yes, and notes T1 next to the item.

43
00:02:40.811 --> 00:02:44.841
Nothing has changed yet, but the account is now spoken for.

44
00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:49.395
T2 happens to reach the grocer first, and that node says yes.

45
00:02:49.395 --> 00:02:53.156
Then it reaches the family's account, which T1 holds.

46
00:02:53.156 --> 00:02:54.646
Does T2 wait in line?

47
00:02:54.646 --> 00:02:55.781
Pause and guess.

48
00:02:58.967 --> 00:02:59.210
No.

49
00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.482
The node rejects it at once.

50
00:03:01.482 --> 00:03:06.432
DynamoDB never makes one transaction wait for another's item.

51
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.038
And one no cancels the whole thing.

52
00:03:09.038 --> 00:03:12.579
Coordinator two tells the grocer's node to forget T2.

53
00:03:12.579 --> 00:03:15.986
Your code gets an error called TransactionConflict.

54
00:03:15.986 --> 00:03:17.991
That's the family's try again.

55
00:03:18.233 --> 00:03:21.324
Meanwhile the bike shop's node says yes to T1.

56
00:03:21.324 --> 00:03:27.907
Everyone has promised, so coordinator one writes T1 commit in the ledger, before anything changes.

57
00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.923
That line is the commit point.

58
00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:33.420
Now suppose coordinator one crashes, right here.

59
00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.295
Last lesson, everything would wait for it.

60
00:03:36.295 --> 00:03:41.567
Here, a recovery program reads the ledger, and hands T1 to coordinator three.

61
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.244
Coordinator three sends the commits.

62
00:03:44.244 --> 00:03:47.843
Each node applies its change and lets go of the item.

63
00:03:47.843 --> 00:03:50.695
The family has 70.00, the bike shop 80.00.

64
00:03:50.967 --> 00:03:54.324
Two rounds, with a write on each item in each round.

65
00:03:54.324 --> 00:03:57.423
So every item in a transaction costs two writes.

66
00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:58.234
And T2?

67
00:03:58.234 --> 00:04:01.589
DynamoDB's library doesn't retry it for you.

68
00:04:01.589 --> 00:04:12.035
Your code waits a few random milliseconds, and sends T2 again, with the same idempotency key, a unique id that makes a repeat do nothing.

69
00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:16.075
The new try checks the balance again: 70 is at least 50.

70
00:04:16.075 --> 00:04:18.388
Everyone says yes, and it commits.

71
00:04:18.388 --> 00:04:21.652
The family has 20.00 left, and the grocer 50.00.

72
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.194
So here's the rule across machines.

73
00:04:24.194 --> 00:04:25.766
Everyone promises first.

74
00:04:25.766 --> 00:04:29.239
One decision is written where a crash can't erase it.

75
00:04:29.239 --> 00:04:31.271
Only then does anything change.

76
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.295
DynamoDB adds two things.

77
00:04:33.295 --> 00:04:36.741
A busy item says no, instead of making you wait.

78
00:04:36.741 --> 00:04:39.541
And any coordinator can finish the job.

79
00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:40.830
It has limits.

80
00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:47.522
One transaction holds up to 100 changes, in one Region, one of Amazon's areas of data centres.

81
00:04:47.522 --> 00:04:53.644
And a busy account means more rejections and more retries, each paying for its writes.

82
00:04:53.644 --> 00:04:57.417
So keep transactions small, and few on the same item.

83
00:04:57.667 --> 00:05:01.252
Think of it as an airline that never keeps a waiting list.

84
00:05:01.252 --> 00:05:03.909
A bit rude, but nobody is ever stuck in it.

85
00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:05.448
One quick check.

86
00:05:05.448 --> 00:05:12.339
T3 wants to take money from the bike shop's account while T1 holds it, prepared but not yet committed.

87
00:05:12.339 --> 00:05:14.704
What does the bike shop's node say?

88
00:05:14.704 --> 00:05:15.785
Pause and think.

89
00:05:18.967 --> 00:05:19.986
No, at once.

90
00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:24.996
T3 is cancelled, and its code tries again after T1 lets go.
